{"name":"Eggs (egg yolk)","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Eggs_in_basket_2020_G1.jpg/960px-Eggs_in_basket_2020_G1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eggs_in_basket_2020_G1.jpg","caption":"Eggs in basket 2020 G1","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":47,"casual":{"lead":["Whole eggs and yolks are a bodybuilding staple, eaten as cheap, complete protein with fat and choline to support muscle gain and a better physique.","The chain runs through overall intake: egg protein scores the maximum on the PDCAAS protein-quality scale, and a hard-boiled egg provides roughly 12.6 g of protein and 294 mg of choline per 100 g. That protein feeds muscle repair and growth alongside training. No published trials were found testing egg yolk specifically for skin, hair or other appearance outcomes."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People eat several eggs a day, boiled, scrambled or in omelettes, mixed into meals for protein and calories during bulks or cuts. Cooked egg protein is more bioavailable than raw."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Research on dietary cholesterol is mixed, and several meta-analyses found no association between moderate egg intake and heart disease in the general population. Raw eggs carry a salmonella risk, and egg allergy is common in infants."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Eggs_in_basket_2020_G1.jpg/960px-Eggs_in_basket_2020_G1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Eggs_in_basket_2020_G1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Eggs in basket 2020 G1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","creator":"George Chernilevsky","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eggs_in_ba